From d10c1aae2bfa28e09ac57a5673521197ce933be3 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Emmanuel Kimathi <kimathi@example.com>
Date: Tue, 25 Aug 2026 01:18:05 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Fix program_code length limit and large-upload performance

Your 800+ row document hit two more real bugs:

1. Invalid program_code 'DIP. PROC. & LOG MNGT' (22 chars) - the
   validator capped program_code at 20 chars, and persistence.py
   truncated it to 20 chars too, but the actual database column
   allows 64. Raised both to match the real schema (also fixed the
   same over-truncation for unit_code and name fields, capped at 100
   instead of the real 255-char limit).

2. Worker timeout + OOM kill - conflict_detector.detect() compared
   every slot in the batch against every other slot (O(n^2) - for
   800 rows that's ~320,000 comparisons), and issued one individual
   INSERT per conflict found. This predates today's changes; it just
   never got exercised at this scale before since earlier bugs
   crashed uploads before reaching this stage.
   - Bucket slots by (term, day_of_week) before comparing pairs,
     since conflicts can only occur within the same term/day -
     avoids comparing across days entirely
   - bulk_create() all conflicts in one query instead of one INSERT
     per conflict
   - persistence.py now caches Term/Department/Program/Unit/Room/
     Lecturer lookups per batch instead of re-querying on every row,
     since the same codes repeat constantly across a real upload.
     Cache writes are deferred until each row's savepoint actually
     commits - if a row fails for an unrelated reason after already
     creating a new Program/Unit, the savepoint rollback undoes that
     creation, so caching it immediately would leave a dangling
     reference that corrupts every later row sharing that code.

Verified against a fresh database with an 800-row batch (repeated
program/unit/room codes, one deliberately broken row partway through
to confirm the rollback-safety fix): 799/800 rows saved correctly in
1.8 seconds (previously timing out past 120s), and the program
involved in the broken row was not duplicated or corrupted.
